@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction
@cindex experimental features
  Tessera X Elfeed prepares context snapshots from feed entries.  The
@samp{x} identifies experimental features; this version supplies
selected-entry and today snapshots.  The snapshot contains metadata and
available text in a separate, read-only buffer, independently of the
search row's appearance.  The buffer in which you start preparation is
called the @dfn{source buffer}; the selected entries form its
@dfn{scope}.

  No Tessera display mode is required, and preparation does not send a
language-model request.  Snapshot size limits and commands for viewing
and discarding snapshots are documented in @ref{Top,,, tessera-x,
Tessera X}.

@node Getting Started
@chapter Getting Started

  Install the package alongside Elfeed and the shared Tessera packages.
Choose whether ordinary selection requests should fetch linked pages.

@menu
* Requirements::        Required packages and loading libraries.
* First Context::       Preparing and viewing a snapshot.
@end menu

@node Requirements
@section Requirements
@cindex installation
  The minimum declared versions are Emacs 30.1, Tessera 0.1.0, Tessera X
0.1.0, and Elfeed 4.0.1.  Tessera also requires Alert 1.2 or later.  For
a source checkout, add @file{lisp/tessera/}, @file{lisp/tessera-x/}, and
@file{lisp/tessera-x-elfeed/} to @code{load-path}, along with Elfeed and
its dependencies.  Load:

@lisp
(require 'tessera-x-elfeed)
@end lisp

  The today command also works in Tree buffers provided by an installed
extension using @code{elfeed-tree-mode}.  Select a line with an
associated Elfeed filter.  Tessera does not provide the Tree interface.

  Use @kbd{M-x customize-group @key{RET} tessera-elfeed @key{RET}} to
configure the options documented here.  You can also load
@code{tessera-elfeed} and set them in your initialization file before
loading the snapshot commands.

@node First Context
@section Preparing a First Context

  In Elfeed Search, move point to an entry and run @kbd{M-x
tessera-x-elfeed-prepare-context}.  The default policy fetches eligible
linked HTTP or HTTPS pages.  To use stored feed bodies only, configure
the following before preparation:

@lisp
(require 'tessera-elfeed)
(setq tessera-x-elfeed-fetch-linked-content nil)
@end lisp

  Once ready, inspect the result with @kbd{M-x tessera-x-show-context}
in the Search buffer.  During an asynchronous fetch, that command can
still show the previous successful snapshot.  @xref{Completion and
Cancellation}.

@node Selecting Entries
@chapter Selecting Entries

  Selection uses Elfeed's native entry records and leaves their tags,
marks, and read state unchanged.

@cindex selection
@deffn Command tessera-x-elfeed-prepare-context
Prepare selected entries in the current Elfeed Search buffer.  Native
marks take precedence over the active region; otherwise select the entry
at point.  Return a context object.  Signal a user error outside Search
or for an empty selection.
@end deffn

  The stored feed body is captured first.  Eligible linked pages may
then be fetched asynchronously according to the options in @ref{Linked
Pages}.  Completion does not visit the entries or mark them read.  There
is no feed subthread command in this version.

@cindex feed grouping
  Records are grouped by feed URL, not merely by feed title.  Feed
groups appear in the order of their first selected entry, and entries
retain input order within each group.  Completing HTTP requests in
another order does not reorder the snapshot.

  Metadata includes the entry URL, feed URL, authors, enclosures with
media types, tags, title, and date.  The printed group label uses the
feed title.  These fields come from stored entries, independently of
abbreviated text in the Search rows.

@node Today Contexts
@chapter Today Contexts

  Today scopes visit the local database and apply native filters.  They
never fetch linked pages or update feeds.

@deffn Command tessera-x-elfeed-prepare-today-context
Prepare today's local entries in the current Search or Tree scope and
return a context object.  In Search, use @code{elfeed-search-filter}.
In Tree, combine the tree filter with the native filter at point.
Signal a user error in unsupported buffers or on a Tree line without a
filter.
@end deffn

  The source buffer supplies the filter:

@table @asis
@item Search buffer
@cindex today, Search filter
Use @code{elfeed-search-filter} without rewriting it or selecting
another buffer.

@item Tree buffer
@cindex today, Tree filter
Combine the Tree view's filter with the filter associated with the
current line.  Select a line with an Elfeed filter before invoking the
command; a line's displayed label alone does not specify a filter.
@end table

  The command visits the database, not just currently displayed Search
rows, using Elfeed's ordinary filter parser and matcher.  It preserves
the filter's restrictions, including its result limit.  An empty scope
produces a ready empty snapshot.

@cindex local day boundaries
  Date selection includes local midnight and excludes the following
midnight.  Native filter restrictions still apply within this interval;
for example, a tag restriction or result limit can make a today snapshot
smaller than all entries dated today.

  Only stored feed bodies are used, regardless of the linked-page
settings.  HTML feed bodies are rendered to text.  Missing stored
content is retained as an item with metadata and a content note.  No
feed update or page download fills the gap.

@node Linked Pages
@chapter Linked Pages

  Linked-page retrieval can replace a selected entry's stored body in
the snapshot.  It does not modify the entry in Elfeed's database.

@menu
* Fetch Eligibility::   Choosing which links to fetch.
* Responses: Responses and Fallback.  Accepted pages and stored-content fallback.
* Transfer Limits::     Timeouts and concurrent downloads.
@end menu

@node Fetch Eligibility
@section Fetch Eligibility
@cindex fetch eligibility
@defopt tessera-x-elfeed-fetch-linked-content
Whether ordinary selected-entry contexts fetch linked pages.  The
initial value is @code{t}.  A @code{nil} value uses stored bodies only.
Today contexts ignore this option and always remain local.
@end defopt

@defopt tessera-x-elfeed-fetch-minimum-characters
A stored-body length at or above which retrieval is skipped, or
@code{nil} to fetch every eligible selected link.  The initial value is
@code{nil}.  A nonnegative integer is measured in characters after HTML
feed content has been rendered to text.
@end defopt

  Only links beginning with @samp{http://} or @samp{https://} enter the
retrieval queue.  With a numeric threshold, a body shorter than that
threshold is eligible; equality skips fetching.  A threshold of zero
therefore skips all linked-page retrieval.  Entries without an eligible
link retain stored content.

@node Responses and Fallback
@section Responses and Fallback
@cindex content types
  Successful responses must have a status in the 200--299 range and a
supported media type: @samp{text/html}, @samp{application/xhtml+xml}, or
@samp{text/plain}.  An absent Content-Type is treated as HTML.  The
declared charset is used when available, with UTF-8 as the fallback for
undecoded response bytes.

  HTML is rendered with SHR; plain text is trimmed at its boundaries.  A
nonempty result replaces the snapshot item's body and receives the note
@samp{Fetched linked page}.  The entry's original provenance and
metadata remain.  This does not run a specialized article extractor;
site navigation can remain in the text.  @xref{HTML Bodies,,, tessera-x,
Tessera X}.

@cindex HTTP fallback
  A startup error, unsupported response type, HTTP failure, empty
result, or timeout retains the stored feed body.  The content note
begins @samp{Using stored feed content:} and records the reason.  One
failed page does not by itself fail the complete snapshot.

  If there was no stored body, fallback still retains the entry
metadata.  Inspect content notes rather than assuming that every
selected link produced a full article.  Attachments and enclosure URLs
remain metadata; their files are not downloaded as snapshot body text.

@node Transfer Limits
@section Transfer Limits
@cindex timeouts and concurrency
@defopt tessera-x-elfeed-fetch-timeout
Seconds allowed for an active linked-page transfer before falling back
to stored content.  Initially @code{15}; the value is a nonnegative
integer.  The timeout begins when the transfer starts, excluding time
spent waiting in the queue.
@end defopt

@defopt tessera-x-elfeed-fetch-concurrency
Maximum active linked-page transfers for one context, initially
@code{4}.  The value is a nonnegative integer; values below one are used
as one.  This is a per-request limit, not a limit shared by all source
buffers.
@end defopt

  Queue eligibility, concurrency, and timeout are captured when the
request is built.  Changing options later does not reconfigure active
requests.  The options can have global or buffer-local values.

  The character budgets apply during final snapshot rendering, after
retrieval.  They are not HTTP transfer limits.  @xref{Character
Budgets,,, tessera-x, Tessera X}.

@node Completion and Cancellation
@chapter Completion and Cancellation
@cindex cancellation
  The snapshot becomes ready after all selected entries have been read
and any linked-page downloads have completed or fallen back to stored
content.  With no pages to fetch, preparation can finish before the
command returns.  Otherwise it continues in the background.

  Starting a newer request in the same source, killing the source, or
changing its major mode cancels pending work.  Explicit cancellation
uses @kbd{M-x tessera-x-cancel-context} in the source buffer.  It stops
pending downloads and prevents the cancelled preparation from replacing
your current snapshot.

  Earlier successful snapshots stay alive until explicitly discarded.
The package installs no default key bindings.  To run Lisp code when a
snapshot becomes ready, see @ref{Consuming Snapshots,,, tessera-x,
Tessera X}.

@node Troubleshooting
@chapter Troubleshooting

  Check the retrieval settings, content notes, and selected filter when
a snapshot contains less text or fewer entries than expected.

@menu
* Stored Content: Stored Content Instead of a Page.  Why a linked page was not used.
* Filters and Timing: Filter and Timing Questions.  Checking entry filters and fetch times.
* Reporting Bugs::      Information to include in a bug report.
@end menu

@node Stored Content Instead of a Page
@section Stored Content Instead of a Page
@cindex stored body fallback
  If a snapshot contains only the feed's summary, inspect its note and
retrieval options.  Fetching may be disabled, the stored body may reach
the threshold, the link may not be HTTP(S), or retrieval may have fallen
back.  Today snapshots always use stored content.

@node Filter and Timing Questions
@section Filter and Timing Questions

  If a today snapshot has fewer entries than expected, check the native
filter, its limit, the selected Tree line, and local calendar dates.  If
retrieval seems slow, check the active-transfer timeout and the number
of queued entries; the timeout applies per transfer, not to the whole
request.

@node Reporting Bugs
@section Reporting Bugs

  For shared hook and buffer-lifetime problems, see
@ref{Troubleshooting,,, tessera-x, Tessera X}.  Report backend issues at
@url{https://github.com/brsvh/emacs-tessera/issues}, including package,
Emacs and Elfeed versions, selection scope, fetch settings, and content
notes sufficient to reproduce the problem.

